// A standalone single-board computer: an ESP32-WROOM-32 on five supply rails
// from one 12 V input, with its own USB programming path, QSPI flash, I2C
// EEPROM, 10/100 Ethernet, a PWM driver, a GPIO expander, a level-shifted
// header and on-board rail and temperature monitoring.
//
// Produced from a prompt through the Synth MCP server.
//
// WHAT THIS BOARD IS FOR. Case 4 of #40: thermal paths, keepouts, assembly
// access and DFM on a board that carries several rails. It is deliberately
// not a fine-pitch board -- escape routing is case 1's question and the
// iCE40 board asks it. The coarsest package here is 0.5 mm pitch (the
// W5500's LQFP-48), which the standard floor clears.
//
// WHY IT IS A COMPUTER AND NOT A RAIL FIXTURE. The earlier revision of this
// board was an ESP32 with two headers: it exercised the rails and nothing
// else, and it needed a second board to program it. This one boots, is
// flashed over its own USB-C connector, stores to its own flash and EEPROM,
// talks to a network, drives sixteen PWM channels and sixteen expander GPIO,
// and reports its own rail voltages and die temperature. Nothing external is
// required to make it run.
//
// THE RAILS, AND WHY THERE ARE FIVE.
//
//   V12       input, through reverse-polarity, a polyfuse and a 15 V clamp
//   V5        MP2307 buck, the only switching stage and the part that gets
//             hot: 500 mW at 50 C/W, with NTC1 beside it
//   V3V3      AMS1117 from V5, the digital rail, poured as a plane
//   V3V3_A    LP5907 behind a ferrite bead -- the ADC reference domain and
//             the temperature sensor, so switching ripple does not reach the
//             rail the board measures itself against
//   V5_SW     TPS22918 load switch under GPIO control, so the peripheral
//             header can be powered down without dropping the module
//
// V5 and V3V3 are planes, each on an inner layer of its own; two rails on one
// layer at the same priority starve each other. The analog rail stays a trace
// because pouring it would couple it back to the switcher it is filtered
// from.
//
// PROCESS. `jlc-advanced`, not the standard tier, and the reason is the
// module rather than the routing: every KiCad ESP32-WROOM-32 footprint drills
// twelve 0.2 mm thermal vias into the ground paddle, below the 0.3 mm
// standard floor, and they sit inside pad 39 so they need the fill as well as
// the drill. Declaring `jlcpcb` here would be declaring a process this board
// cannot be built on.
//
// THERMAL. Three parts declare what they dissipate and how well they shed it.
// The buck is the one that matters: its exposed pad is its heat path and ties
// to ground, NTC1 sits beside it, and U13 reports the board temperature over
// I2C, so the thermal claim is measured at runtime rather than asserted here.
//
// KEEPOUT. The module's antenna is at one end of the package, not its centre,
// and copper must not sit under it. The keepout is placed on that end.
//
// ASSEMBLY ACCESS. Both connectors, the RJ45, both headers and the five test
// points are hinted to edges; the mounting holes take the corners. A test
// point in the middle of a populated board is a test point you cannot reach.
//
// THE MODULE'S RESERVED PINS. Pads 17 to 22 are the module's own SPI flash
// bus, bonded to the flash die inside the shield, and pad 32 is marked NC.
// They are modelled `do_not_connect` and this board connects none of them;
// the external flash at U7 is on HSPI instead. All four ground pads -- 1, 15,
// 38 and the paddle at 39 -- are connected, because the footprint has four.

board "multirail_compute" {
    layers 6
    manufacturer "jlc-advanced"
    revision "B"

    // Two independent reasons this board cannot be built without a filled
    // via, which is why it says so here rather than taking a CLI flag:
    //
    // The module's paddle. Every KiCad ESP32-WROOM-32 footprint drills
    // twelve 0.2 mm thermal vias inside pad 39, and a via inside an SMD pad
    // is a solder-bridging defect on a process that cannot fill and cap it.
    //
    // The plane pads. V5 and V3V3 are poured planes, and a plane pad needs a
    // stitching via beside it to reach its layer. On a board this dense some
    // pads have no legal position beside them -- every candidate is inside
    // another pad's clearance -- and the only place left for the via is the
    // pad itself.
    //
    // `jlc-advanced` offers the fill, so ERC accepts the requirement; it
    // would refuse a tier that does not.
    requires via_in_pad

    // Six layers, not four. The Ethernet MDI pairs need a continuous
    // reference under them for their whole run, and the two plane rails each
    // want a layer, which on four layers leaves the pairs referenced to a
    // split. Top and bottom carry signals over ground pours; In1 and In4 are
    // ground; In2 and In3 are the rails.
    // The outer dielectric is 0.2 mm rather than the 0.1 mm a four-layer
    // board would use, and that is an impedance decision rather than a
    // mechanical one. A 100 ohm differential pair over a plane 0.1 mm away
    // needs a track near the 0.127 mm process floor, which leaves no
    // tolerance; at 0.2 mm the same pair is a 0.25 mm track, comfortably
    // above the floor. The core makes the total up to 1.59 mm.
    stackup {
      copper 0.035mm
      insulator 0.2mm er 4.4 material "FR4"
      copper 0.0152mm
      insulator 0.2mm er 4.4 material "FR4"
      copper 0.0152mm
      insulator 0.66mm er 4.6 material "FR4"
      copper 0.0152mm
      insulator 0.2mm er 4.4 material "FR4"
      copper 0.0152mm
      insulator 0.2mm er 4.4 material "FR4"
      copper 0.035mm
    }

    // `plane` floods a zone for the class's nets on the inner layers. A
    // netclass assigns no nets by itself: each is joined by `class "NAME"` on
    // a `connect`, which is why every rail connect below carries one.
    netclass "V5" {
      trace_width 0.8mm
      clearance 0.25mm
      plane
    }

    netclass "V3V3" {
      trace_width 0.6mm
      clearance 0.25mm
      plane
    }

    // The analog rail stays a trace: it is behind a ferrite bead, and
    // pouring it as a plane would couple it back to the switching rail it is
    // filtered from.
    netclass "ANALOG" {
      trace_width 0.4mm
      clearance 0.3mm
    }

    // 100BASE-TX is a 100 ohm differential interface. The pair budget is per
    // pair, on the `diff_pair` blocks at the foot of the file; this bounds
    // the spread across all four MDI nets, so transmit and receive leave the
    // controller together.
    netclass "MDI" {
      trace_width 0.25mm
      clearance 0.2mm
      max_skew 0.5mm
    }

    // One SPI bus, three devices, three chip selects. It used to be two
    // buses: the flash had HSPI to itself. HSPI is IO12-15, which is also
    // where the ESP32's JTAG port is fixed in silicon, so a dedicated flash
    // bus and a debug port cannot both exist. The flash joins the Ethernet
    // controller and the card socket here, and IO12-15 become JTAG.
    netclass "SPI_BUS" {
      trace_width 0.2mm
      clearance 0.2mm
      max_skew 2.0mm
    }

    // The two field buses. Both are differential pairs into screw-terminal
    // wiring, so they are routed as pairs and clamped at the connector.
    netclass "FIELDBUS" {
      trace_width 0.25mm
      clearance 0.25mm
    }

    group "POWER IN" color "#c2410c" {
      component J1: connector "jst_ph_2pin" {
        placement_hint { region: left_edge edge: left priority: hard }
      }
      component F1: fuse      "polyfuse"
      component D1: diode     "d_schottky"
      // The clamp the 12 V input never had. A TVS conducts nothing below its
      // standoff and everything above its clamping voltage: 15 V standoff
      // clears a 12 V nominal with regulation margin, and the 24.4 V clamp
      // is under the MP2307's 28 V input rating. The part that used to sit
      // here was a 5 V array, which on a 12 V rail clamps continuously and
      // sinks the fuse's current instead of a transient's.
      component D2: diode     "smaj15a_tvs"
      component C1: capacitor "c_electrolytic_100uf" value "100uF"
      component TP1: testpoint "testpoint_pad_1_5" {
        placement_hint { region: top_edge edge: top priority: soft }
      }

      // Reverse polarity first, then the fuse, then the clamp. The clamp sits
      // behind the fuse so a sustained overvoltage opens the fuse rather than
      // cooking the diode.
      connect J1.p1 -> D1.anode
      connect D1.cathode -> F1.p1
      connect F1.p2 -> C1.p1 as "V12"
      connect F1.p2 -> D2.cathode
      connect D2.anode -> C1.p2 as "GND"
      connect F1.p2 -> TP1.p1
      connect J1.p2 -> C1.p2 as "GND"
    }

    group "5V BUCK" color "#b91c1c" {
      component U1: regulator "mp2307_buck" {
        placement_hint { region: left_edge priority: soft }
      }
      component L1: inductor  "l_generic_1210" value "10uH"
      component C2: capacitor "c_generic_0805" value "22uF"
      component C3: capacitor "c_generic_0805" value "22uF"
      component R1: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "31.6k"
      component R2: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "10k"
      component C4: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF"
      // The thermal sense, beside the part that dissipates. R39 is the fixed
      // half of the divider and NTC1 the varying half; the tap between them
      // is what an ADC pin can actually read. Wired straight from the rail to
      // ground, as it was, a thermistor is a 2.5 mA load that reports
      // nothing.
      component NTC1: resistor "r_thermistor_ntc_10k" value "10k"
      component R39: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "10k"
      // The compensation network, the soft-start cap and the enable pull-up.
      component R9: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "6.8k"
      component C12: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "3.9nF"
      component C14: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF"
      component R10: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "100k"
      component TP2: testpoint "testpoint_pad_1_5" {
        placement_hint { region: top_edge edge: top priority: soft }
      }

      connect R45.p2 -> U1.in
      connect R45.p2 -> C2.p1
      connect C2.p2 -> U1.gnd as "GND"
      // EN through 100k, not straight to 12 V: absolute maximum on every pin
      // but IN, SW and BS is 6 V, and the part has a 7 V zener on EN that the
      // resistor is there to feed.
      connect U1.en -> R10.p1
      connect R10.p2 -> R45.p2
      connect U1.bs -> C4.p1
      connect C4.p2 -> U1.sw
      connect U1.sw -> L1.p1
      connect L1.p2 -> C3.p1 as "V5" class "V5"
      connect L1.p2 -> R1.p1
      connect R1.p2 -> U1.fb
      connect U1.fb -> R2.p1
      connect R2.p2 -> U1.gnd as "GND"
      connect C3.p2 -> U1.gnd as "GND"
      connect L1.p2 -> TP2.p1
      // The exposed pad is the heat path and is tied to ground; an untied one
      // is both a thermal failure and unconnected copper under the package.
      connect U1.ep -> U1.gnd as "GND"
      connect L1.p2 -> R39.p1
      connect R39.p2 -> NTC1.p1 as "NTC_SENSE"
      connect NTC1.p2 -> U1.gnd as "GND"
      // Series RC from COMP to ground, and the soft-start cap. 0.1 uF gives
      // the datasheet's 15 ms ramp.
      connect U1.comp -> R9.p1
      connect R9.p2 -> C12.p1
      connect C12.p2 -> U1.gnd as "GND"
      connect U1.ss -> C14.p1
      connect C14.p2 -> U1.gnd as "GND"
    }

    // The digital rail is a switcher, not a linear, and the arithmetic is why.
    // Summed, the parts on V3V3 draw 320 mA with the Ethernet island on its
    // own supply. An AMS1117 dropping 5 V to 3.3 V at that current dissipates
    // 1.7 V x 0.32 A = 0.54 W in a SOT-223, and at the 620 mA this rail
    // carried before the island was split off it was 1.05 W -- 25 + 1.05 x 60
    // = 88 C, past the 85 C the thermal rule warns at, in the one package on
    // the board with no exposed pad. A second MP2307 loses about 0.23 W at
    // the same current, which is 37 C, and brings 800 mA of headroom instead
    // of a thermal limit.
    group "3V3 RAIL" color "#0369a1" {
      component U2: regulator "mp2307_buck"
      component L2: inductor  "l_generic_1210" value "10uH"
      component C5: capacitor "c_generic_0805" value "22uF"
      component C6: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF"
      component C39: capacitor "c_generic_0805" value "22uF"
      component C41: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF"
      component R41: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "25.5k"
      component R42: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "10k"
      component R43: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "6.8k"
      component C42: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "3.9nF"
      component C43: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF"
      component R44: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "100k"
      component TP3: testpoint "testpoint_pad_1_5" {
        placement_hint { region: top_edge edge: top priority: soft }
      }

      connect L1.p2 -> U2.in
      connect L1.p2 -> C39.p1
      connect C39.p2 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      // EN through 100k from the 5 V rail. Unlike the 12 V stage this input
      // is already inside the part's 6 V maximum, so the resistor is feeding
      // the pin's zener rather than protecting it.
      connect U2.en -> R44.p1
      connect R44.p2 -> L1.p2
      connect U2.bs -> C41.p1
      connect C41.p2 -> U2.sw
      connect U2.sw -> L2.p1
      connect L2.p2 -> C5.p1 as "V3V3" class "V3V3"
      // 0.925 V reference, so 25.5k over 10k sets 3.29 V. The divider taps
      // the output after the inductor, not the switch node.
      connect L2.p2 -> R41.p1
      connect R41.p2 -> U2.fb
      connect U2.fb -> R42.p1
      connect R42.p2 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect C5.p2 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect L2.p2 -> C6.p1
      connect C6.p2 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect L2.p2 -> TP3.p1
      connect U2.ep -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U2.comp -> R43.p1
      connect R43.p2 -> C42.p1
      connect C42.p2 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U2.ss -> C43.p1
      connect C43.p2 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
    }

    group "3V3 ANALOG" color "#047857" {
      // The bead is what makes this a separate rail rather than a second
      // name for the digital one.
      component FB1: inductor  "ferrite_bead_0603" value "600R"
      component U3: regulator  "lp5907_3v3"
      component C7: capacitor  "c_generic_0603" value "1uF"
      component C8: capacitor  "c_generic_0603" value "1uF"
      component TP4: testpoint "testpoint_pad_1_5" {
        placement_hint { region: bottom_edge edge: bottom priority: soft }
      }

      connect L2.p2 -> FB1.p1
      connect FB1.p2 -> U3.vin
      connect U3.vin -> C7.p1
      connect C7.p2 -> U3.gnd as "GND"
      connect U3.en -> U3.vin
      connect U3.vout -> C8.p1 as "V3V3_A" class "ANALOG"
      connect C8.p2 -> U3.gnd as "GND"
      connect U3.vout -> TP4.p1
    }

    group "SWITCHED RAIL" color "#7c3aed" {
      component U4: regulator "tps22918_loadswitch"
      component C9: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "1uF"
      component R3: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "100k"
      component TP5: testpoint "testpoint_pad_1_5" {
        placement_hint { region: bottom_edge edge: bottom priority: soft }
      }

      // From V5, not V3V3: this part's input range starts at 3.5 V, so a
      // 3.3 V supply is below it and E-SYNTH-POWER-009 says so.
      connect L1.p2 -> U4.vin
      connect U4.vout -> C9.p1 as "V5_SW" class "ANALOG"
      connect C9.p2 -> U4.gnd as "GND"
      // `ct` sets the inrush slew. `on` reaches a GPIO rather than a pull-up:
      // the rail is switched because software switches it, which is the whole
      // point of having it. The earlier revision strapped this high for want
      // of a spare pin, which made it a rail with a switch in it.
      connect U4.ct -> R3.p1
      connect R3.p2 -> U4.gnd as "GND"
      connect U4.vout -> TP5.p1
    }

    group "COMPUTE" color "#4338ca" {
      component U5: mcu "esp32_wroom_32" {
        placement_hint { region: centre priority: hard }
      }
      component C10: capacitor "c_generic_0805" value "22uF"
      component C11: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF"
      component R4: resistor   "r_generic_0603" value "10k"
      component C15: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF"
      component R11: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "10k"
      component SW1: switch    "spst_tactile"
      component SW2: switch    "spst_tactile"

      connect L2.p2 -> U5.vdd
      // All four ground pads. The footprint has four and the paddle at 39 is
      // the module's only thermal path.
      connect U5.gnd -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U5.gnd_2 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U5.gnd_3 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U5.gnd_4 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U5.vdd -> C10.p1
      connect C10.p2 -> U5.gnd as "GND"
      connect U5.vdd -> C11.p1
      connect C11.p2 -> U5.gnd as "GND"
      // EN: 10k pull-up and 100 nF to ground is the module's reset delay,
      // and SW1 pulls it down.
      connect U5.en -> R4.p1
      connect R4.p2 -> U5.vdd
      connect U5.en -> C15.p1
      connect C15.p2 -> U5.gnd as "GND"
      connect U5.en -> SW1.p1
      connect SW1.p2 -> U5.gnd as "GND"
      // IO0 low at reset enters the download mode the USB path drives.
      connect U5.io0 -> R11.p1
      connect R11.p2 -> U5.vdd
      connect U5.io0 -> SW2.p1
      connect SW2.p2 -> U5.gnd as "GND"
      // The switched rail's enable, from the expander: it is a slow control
      // line and the module has no pin to spare for it.
      connect U10.gpa5 -> U4.on as "PERIPH_EN"
    }

    group "USB AND PROGRAMMING" color "#0d9488" {
      component J2: connector "usb_c_receptacle" {
        placement_hint { region: bottom_edge edge: bottom priority: hard }
      }
      component U6: ic         "ch340g"
      component Y1: crystal    "ecs_120_20_33_tr"
      component C16: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF"
      component C17: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF"
      component D3: diode      "esd_usb"
      component D4: diode      "esd_usb"
      component R16: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "5.1k"
      component R17: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "5.1k"
      component Q1: transistor "npn_bjt_2n3904"
      component Q2: transistor "npn_bjt_2n3904"
      component R12: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "10k"
      component R13: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "10k"
      component D5: diode      "esd_usb"
      component D6: diode      "esd_usb"

      // A sink, not a host: VBUS enters here and both CC lines carry the
      // 5.1k Rd that advertises a UFP.
      connect J2.cc1 -> R16.p1
      connect R16.p2 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect J2.cc1 -> D5.io
      connect D5.gnd -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect J2.cc2 -> R17.p1
      connect R17.p2 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect J2.cc2 -> D6.io
      connect D6.gnd -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect J2.gnd -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect J2.shld -> U2.gnd as "GND"

      // The bridge runs from the 3.3 V rail, so V3 ties to VCC and the logic
      // levels match the module without a shifter.
      connect L2.p2 -> U6.vcc
      connect U6.v3 -> U6.vcc
      connect U6.vcc -> C16.p1
      connect C16.p2 -> U6.gnd as "GND"
      connect U6.gnd -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U6.xi -> Y1.xtal_a
      connect U6.xo -> Y1.xtal_b
      connect Y1.gnd_2 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect Y1.gnd_4 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U6.vcc -> C17.p1
      connect C17.p2 -> U6.gnd as "GND"

      // The data pair, clamped at the connector.
      connect J2.dp -> U6.ud_plus as "USB_DP"
      connect J2.dn -> U6.ud_minus as "USB_DN"
      connect J2.dp -> D3.io
      connect D3.gnd -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect J2.dn -> D4.io
      connect D4.gnd -> U2.gnd as "GND"

      // The console, crossed: the bridge's TXD drives the module's RX.
      connect U6.txd -> U5.io3_rx as "UART_RX"
      connect U6.rxd -> U5.io1_tx as "UART_TX"

      // Auto-reset. DTR and RTS through two transistors put the module into
      // download mode without a button press, which is what makes this board
      // flashable from a host rather than from a second board. The pair is
      // cross-coupled so that asserting one line alone does nothing.
      // The modem status inputs this console does not use, asserted rather
      // than floating, and R232 low for plain UART framing.
      connect U6.cts -> U6.gnd as "GND"
      connect U6.dsr -> U6.gnd as "GND"
      connect U6.ri -> U6.gnd as "GND"
      connect U6.dcd -> U6.gnd as "GND"
      connect U6.r232 -> U6.gnd as "GND"

      connect U6.dtr -> R12.p1
      connect R12.p2 -> Q1.base
      connect Q1.emitter -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect Q1.collector -> U5.en
      connect U6.rts -> R13.p1
      connect R13.p2 -> Q2.base
      connect Q2.emitter -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect Q2.collector -> U5.io0
    }

    group "STORAGE" color "#a16207" {
      component U7: memory    "w25q128_flash"
      component C20: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF"
      component R18: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "10k"
      component R22: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "10k"
      component R23: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "10k"
      component U8: memory    "24lc256_eeprom"
      component C21: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF"

      // HSPI, not the module's own flash bus. Pads 17-22 are bonded to the
      // die inside the shield and carry `do_not_connect`.
      connect L2.p2 -> U7.vcc
      connect U7.gnd -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U7.vcc -> C20.p1
      connect C20.p2 -> U7.gnd as "GND"
      // On the shared bus, with its own chip select. IO12-15 are JTAG now.
      connect U7.clk -> U5.io18 as "SPI_SCK" class "SPI_BUS"
      connect U7.io0 -> U5.io23 as "SPI_MOSI" class "SPI_BUS"
      connect U7.io1 -> U5.io19 as "SPI_MISO" class "SPI_BUS"
      connect U5.io25 -> U7.cs as "FLASH_CS" class "SPI_BUS"
      // CS idles high so the part ignores the bus while the module boots.
      connect U7.cs -> R18.p1
      connect R18.p2 -> L2.p2
      // The quad lines are held high through resistors, not strapped: in SPI
      // mode they are /WP and /HOLD and a floating /HOLD stalls the part, but
      // tying a bidirectional pin hard to a rail forbids ever driving it.
      connect U7.io2 -> R22.p1
      connect R22.p2 -> L2.p2
      connect U7.io3 -> R23.p1
      connect R23.p2 -> L2.p2

      // Board identity and configuration, on the I2C bus at address 0x50.
      connect L2.p2 -> U8.vdd
      connect U8.gnd -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U8.vdd -> C21.p1
      connect C21.p2 -> U8.gnd as "GND"
      connect U5.io21_sda -> U8.sda as "I2C_SDA"
      connect U5.io22_scl -> U8.scl as "I2C_SCL"
      // Address strapped to 000, and write-protect tied low so the board can
      // write its own configuration.
      connect U8.a0 -> U8.gnd as "GND"
      connect U8.a1 -> U8.gnd as "GND"
      connect U8.a2 -> U8.gnd as "GND"
      connect U8.wp -> U8.gnd as "GND"
    }

    group "ETHERNET" color "#1d4ed8" {
      component U15: regulator "ams1117_3v3"
      component C37: capacitor "c_generic_0805" value "22uF"
      component C38: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF"
      component C40: capacitor "c_generic_0805" value "22uF"
      component TP6: testpoint "testpoint_pad_1_5" {
        placement_hint { region: right_edge edge: right priority: soft }
      }
      component U9: ic        "w5500_ethernet" {
        placement_hint { region: right_edge priority: soft }
      }
      component J3: connector "rj45_magjack_si60062" {
        placement_hint { region: right_edge edge: right priority: hard }
      }
      component Y2: crystal   "osc_smd_25mhz"
      component C22: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF"
      component C23: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF"
      component C24: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF"
      component C25: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "1uF"
      component C26: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF"
      component C27: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF"
      component C28: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF"
      component R20: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "12.4k"
      component R21: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "10k"
      component LED3: led      "led_green_0603"
      component R25: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "1k"
      component LED4: led      "led_amber_0603"
      component R26: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "1k"
      component C29: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF"
      component C30: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF"

      // The island's own rail, from V5 rather than from V3V3, so the
      // controller's 100 mA of switching current does not return through the
      // module's supply.
      connect L1.p2 -> U15.vin
      connect U15.vin -> C40.p1
      connect C40.p2 -> U15.gnd as "GND"
      connect U15.vout -> C37.p1 as "V3V3_E" class "V3V3"
      connect C37.p2 -> U15.gnd as "GND"
      connect U15.vout -> C38.p1
      connect C38.p2 -> U15.gnd as "GND"
      connect U15.vout -> TP6.p1

      connect U15.vout -> U9.vdd
      connect U9.gnd -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U9.gnd_2 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U9.vdd -> C22.p1
      connect C22.p2 -> U9.gnd as "GND"

      // The analog island. The controller's AVDD pads take the digital rail
      // through their own decoupling, and every AGND pad reaches the one
      // ground: a split return under a 25 MHz part is a worse problem than
      // the coupling it is meant to avoid.
      connect U15.vout -> U9.avdd
      connect U9.avdd -> C23.p1
      connect C23.p2 -> U9.gnd as "GND"
      connect U15.vout -> U9.avdd_2
      connect U15.vout -> U9.avdd_3
      connect U15.vout -> U9.avdd_4
      connect U15.vout -> U9.avdd_5
      connect U15.vout -> U9.avdd_6
      connect U9.avdd_6 -> C24.p1
      connect C24.p2 -> U9.gnd as "GND"
      connect U9.agnd -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U9.agnd_2 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U9.agnd_3 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U9.agnd_4 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U9.agnd_5 -> U2.gnd as "GND"

      // EXRES1 sets the PHY's bias current and the datasheet fixes it at
      // 12.4k to ground; VBG and TOCAP are internal references that want
      // their own capacitor, and 1V2O is the core regulator's output.
      connect U9.exres1 -> R20.p1
      connect R20.p2 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U9.vbg -> C26.p1
      connect C26.p2 -> U9.gnd as "GND"
      connect U9.tocap -> C27.p1
      connect C27.p2 -> U9.gnd as "GND"
      connect U9.core_1v2 -> C25.p1
      connect C25.p2 -> U9.gnd as "GND"

      // 25 MHz, from an oscillator rather than a crystal: the oscillator is
      // a driven output, so XO stays unconnected.
      connect U15.vout -> Y2.vcc
      connect Y2.gnd -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect Y2.vcc -> C28.p1
      connect C28.p2 -> Y2.gnd as "GND"
      connect Y2.out -> U9.xi_clkin as "ETH_CLK"
      connect Y2.oe -> Y2.vcc

      // SPI mode: all three PMODE pins high selects the hardwired TCP/IP
      // stack over the variants that expect a different host interface.
      connect U9.pmode0 -> U15.vout
      connect U9.pmode1 -> U15.vout
      connect U9.pmode2 -> U15.vout

      // VSPI from the module, with interrupt and reset on their own pins.
      connect U9.sclk -> U5.io18 as "SPI_SCK" class "SPI_BUS"
      connect U9.mosi -> U5.io23 as "SPI_MOSI" class "SPI_BUS"
      connect U9.miso -> U5.io19 as "SPI_MISO" class "SPI_BUS"
      connect U5.io5 -> U9.scs_n as "ETH_CS" class "SPI_BUS"
      connect U5.io4 -> U9.intn as "ETH_INT"
      connect U5.io16 -> U9.rst_n as "ETH_RST"
      connect U9.rst_n -> R21.p1
      connect R21.p2 -> U15.vout

      // The MDI pairs, into a jack that carries its own magnetics. The
      // centre taps take a capacitor to ground, which is what holds the
      // pair's common mode; floating, it wanders.
      connect U9.txp -> J3.td_p as "ETH_TXP" class "MDI"
      connect U9.txn -> J3.td_n as "ETH_TXN" class "MDI"
      connect U9.rxp -> J3.rd_p as "ETH_RXP" class "MDI"
      connect U9.rxn -> J3.rd_n as "ETH_RXN" class "MDI"
      connect J3.tct -> C29.p1
      connect C29.p2 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect J3.rct -> C30.p1
      connect C30.p2 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect J3.gnd -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect J3.shield -> U2.gnd as "GND"

      // Link and activity, driven by the controller's own LED outputs.
      connect U9.linkled -> R25.p1
      connect R25.p2 -> LED3.anode
      connect LED3.cathode -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U9.actled -> R26.p1
      connect R26.p2 -> LED4.anode
      connect LED4.cathode -> U2.gnd as "GND"
    }

    group "IO EXPANSION" color "#0891b2" {
      component U10: ic        "mcp23017_gpio"
      component C31: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF"
      component R27: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "4.7k"
      component R28: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "4.7k"
      component U11: ic        "pca9685_pwm"
      component C32: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF"
      component U12: level_shifter "txs0108e"
      component C33: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF"
      component C34: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF"

      // The expander: sixteen GPIO at address 0x20, with reset and interrupt
      // on module pins rather than strapped.
      connect L2.p2 -> U10.vdd
      connect U10.v_ss -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U10.vdd -> C31.p1
      connect C31.p2 -> U10.v_ss as "GND"
      connect U5.io21_sda -> U10.sda as "I2C_SDA"
      connect U5.io22_scl -> U10.sck as "I2C_SCL"
      connect U10.a0 -> U10.v_ss as "GND"
      connect U10.a1 -> U10.v_ss as "GND"
      connect U10.a2 -> U10.v_ss as "GND"
      connect U5.io17 -> U10.reset as "EXP_RESET"
      connect U5.io27 -> U10.inta as "EXP_INT"

      // The one bus pull-up pair for the whole I2C bus. Four devices sit on
      // it -- the EEPROM, the expander, the PWM driver and the sensor -- and
      // one pair of pull-ups is what a shared bus wants.
      connect U10.sda -> R27.p1
      connect R27.p2 -> L2.p2
      connect U10.sck -> R28.p1
      connect R28.p2 -> L2.p2

      // Sixteen PWM channels at address 0x40, output-enabled from a GPIO so
      // the outputs are inert until software asks for them.
      connect L2.p2 -> U11.vdd
      connect U11.vss -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U11.vdd -> C32.p1
      connect C32.p2 -> U11.vss as "GND"
      connect U5.io21_sda -> U11.sda as "I2C_SDA"
      connect U5.io22_scl -> U11.scl as "I2C_SCL"
      connect U11.a0 -> U11.vss as "GND"
      connect U11.a1 -> U11.vss as "GND"
      connect U11.a2 -> U11.vss as "GND"
      connect U11.a3 -> U11.vss as "GND"
      connect U11.a4 -> U11.vss as "GND"
      connect U11.a5 -> U11.vss as "GND"
      connect U11.extclk -> U11.vss as "GND"
      connect U10.gpa3 -> U11.oe as "PWM_OE"

      // The level shifter gives the header a 5 V side. A is the module's
      // 3.3 V, B is the switched 5 V rail, so a 5 V peripheral can be driven
      // and powered down together.
      connect L2.p2 -> U12.vcca
      connect U4.vout -> U12.vccb
      connect U12.gnd -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U12.vcca -> C33.p1
      connect C33.p2 -> U12.gnd as "GND"
      connect U12.vccb -> C34.p1
      connect C34.p2 -> U12.gnd as "GND"
      connect U12.oe -> U12.vcca
      connect U10.gpa0 -> U12.a1
      connect U10.gpa1 -> U12.a2
    }

    group "MONITORING" color "#065f46" {
      component U13: sensor   "tmp117_temperature"
      component C35: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF"
      component R40: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "10k"
      component U16: sensor    "ina3221_power_monitor"
      component C44: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF"
      component R45: resistor  "r_generic_2512" value "0.01"

      // The sensor runs from the analog rail, because a board that measures
      // its own temperature should not read the switcher's ripple as a
      // reading. Address strapped to 0x48, alert on a module pin.
      connect U3.vout -> U13.vdd
      connect U13.gnd -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U13.vdd -> C35.p1
      connect C35.p2 -> U13.gnd as "GND"
      connect U5.io21_sda -> U13.sda as "I2C_SDA"
      connect U5.io22_scl -> U13.scl as "I2C_SCL"
      connect U13.add0 -> U13.gnd as "GND"
      connect U10.gpa4 -> U13.alert as "TEMP_ALERT"
      // ALERT is open-drain: without a pull-up it can only ever pull low
      // against nothing. The pull-up goes to V3V3 rather than to the analog
      // rail the sensor runs from, because the pin that reads this line is
      // the module's, and an open-drain level is referenced to the receiver.
      connect U13.alert -> R40.p1
      connect R40.p2 -> L2.p2

      // Three-channel power telemetry. The device runs from V3V3 and its
      // common mode reaches 26 V, so the 12 V input and both regulated rails
      // are measurable on one part at one I2C address.
      connect L2.p2 -> U16.vs
      connect U16.gnd -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U16.ep -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U16.vs -> C44.p1
      connect C44.p2 -> U16.gnd as "GND"
      connect U5.io21_sda -> U16.sda as "I2C_SDA"
      connect U5.io22_scl -> U16.scl as "I2C_SCL"
      // A0 to VS, not to ground. Grounded this part answers at 0x40, which
      // is exactly where the PWM driver with all six of its address pins low
      // already answers. Tied to VS it is 0x41. The four options are GND,
      // VS, SDA and SCL for 0x40 through 0x43.
      connect U16.a0 -> U16.vs
      connect L2.p2 -> U16.vpu
      connect U10.gpb0 -> U16.critical_n as "RAIL_CRITICAL"

      // Channel 1 measures the 12 V input through a 10 mohm shunt, which is
      // the current worth knowing: everything the board draws passes through
      // it. 2 A gives 20 mV across the shunt and 40 mW in a 2512.
      connect F1.p2 -> R45.p1
      connect R45.p1 -> U16.in1_p
      connect R45.p2 -> U16.in1_n
      // Channels 2 and 3 are bus-voltage only, inputs tied together at the
      // rail. A shunt in series with V5 or V3V3 would split a poured plane
      // in two, which costs more in return path than the reading is worth;
      // channel 1 already bounds the board's total draw.
      connect L1.p2 -> U16.in2_p
      connect L1.p2 -> U16.in2_n
      connect L2.p2 -> U16.in3_p
      connect L2.p2 -> U16.in3_n
      // The buck's thermistor tap. 10k over a 10k NTC puts 2.5 V at 25 C and
      // falls as the regulator warms, so the firmware reads the rise at the
      // part rather than inferring it from the die sensor across the board.
      connect U5.sensor_vn -> R39.p2 as "NTC_SENSE"

      // Rail monitoring. Two dividers onto the module's two input-only ADC
      // pins: 100k/10k brings a 12 V rail to 1.09 V and 22k/10k brings 5 V
      // to 1.56 V, both inside the ADC's range. These pins have no output
      // driver, which is why the dividers go here and not on a GPIO.
      // Rail monitoring is U16's job now: it measures current as well as
      // voltage, over I2C, which frees the module's two input-only ADC pins
      // for the field buses' receive lines. The thermistor stays an ADC
      // measurement because it is a resistance and nothing on the bus reads
      // one.
    }

    group "STATUS AND DISPLAY" color "#be123c" {
      component U14: display  "ssd1306_oled" {
        placement_hint { region: top_edge edge: top priority: soft }
      }
      component LED1: led      "led_green_0603"
      component R36: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "1k"
      component LED2: led      "led_blue_0603"
      component R37: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "1k"
      component SW3: switch    "spst_tactile"
      component R38: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "10k"

      // The status display sits on the shared I2C bus at 0x3C.
      connect L2.p2 -> U14.vdd
      connect U14.gnd -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U5.io21_sda -> U14.sda as "I2C_SDA"
      connect U5.io22_scl -> U14.scl as "I2C_SCL"

      // Power on, and a user LED the firmware drives.
      connect L2.p2 -> R36.p1
      connect R36.p2 -> LED1.anode
      connect LED1.cathode -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U10.gpa2 -> R37.p1
      connect R37.p2 -> LED2.anode
      connect LED2.cathode -> U2.gnd as "GND"

      // A user button on an input-only pin, which therefore needs its
      // pull-up on the board rather than in the pad.
      connect U5.io34 -> R38.p1
      connect R38.p2 -> L2.p2
      connect U5.io34 -> SW3.p1
      connect SW3.p2 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
    }

    group "EXPANSION HEADERS" color "#be185d" {
      component J4: connector "header_1x20" {
        placement_hint { region: top_edge edge: top priority: hard }
      }
      component J5: connector "header_1x20" {
        placement_hint { region: bottom_edge edge: bottom priority: hard }
      }

      // The digital header: the switched rail, the shifted 5 V pair, the
      // console, the shared I2C bus and eight expander lines.
      connect U4.vout -> J4.p1
      connect J4.p2 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U12.b1 -> J4.p3
      connect U12.b2 -> J4.p4
      connect U5.io1_tx -> J4.p5
      connect U5.io3_rx -> J4.p6
      connect U5.io21_sda -> J4.p7
      connect U5.io22_scl -> J4.p8
      connect U10.gpb2 -> J4.p9
      connect U10.gpb3 -> J4.p10
      connect U10.gpb4 -> J4.p11
      connect U10.gpb5 -> J4.p12
      connect U10.gpb6 -> J4.p13
      connect U10.gpb7 -> J4.p14
      // The expander's sixteen lines are now fully spoken for: GPA0-GPA1 to
      // the level shifter, GPA2-GPA5 to the user LED, the PWM output-enable,
      // the temperature alert and the switched rail, GPA6-GPA7 and GPB0-GPB1
      // to the card detect, the RTC alarm, the monitor's critical flag and
      // the watchdog heartbeat, and GPB2-GPB7 to this header. So p15-p20
      // carry the field buses and the console instead of more expander pins.
      connect U18.ro -> J4.p15 as "RS485_RX"
      connect U18.di -> J4.p16 as "RS485_TX"
      connect U17.r -> J4.p17 as "CAN_RX"
      connect U17.d -> J4.p18 as "CAN_TX"
      connect L2.p2 -> J4.p19
      connect J4.p20 -> U2.gnd as "GND"

      // The analog and drive header: the filtered rail, a spare ADC pin and
      // the first twelve PWM channels.
      connect U3.vout -> J5.p1
      connect J5.p2 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U5.sensor_vn -> J5.p3
      connect U5.io2 -> J5.p4
      connect U11.led0 -> J5.p5
      connect U11.led1 -> J5.p6
      connect U11.led2 -> J5.p7
      connect U11.led3 -> J5.p8
      connect U11.led4 -> J5.p9
      connect U11.led5 -> J5.p10
      connect U11.led6 -> J5.p11
      connect U11.led7 -> J5.p12
      connect U11.led8 -> J5.p13
      connect U11.led9 -> J5.p14
      connect U11.led10 -> J5.p15
      connect U11.led11 -> J5.p16
      connect U11.led12 -> J5.p17
      connect U11.led13 -> J5.p18
      connect U11.led14 -> J5.p19
      connect U11.led15 -> J5.p20
    }

    group "REMOVABLE STORAGE" color "#7e22ce" {
      component J8: connector "microsd_dm3at" {
        placement_hint { region: right_edge edge: right priority: hard }
      }
      component C47: capacitor "c_generic_0805" value "10uF"
      component C48: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF"
      component R53: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "10k"
      component R54: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "10k"
      component R55: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "10k"

      // On the shared bus with its own chip select, in one-bit SPI mode:
      // CMD is the host's MOSI, DAT0 the card's MISO, DAT3 the select. The
      // card is a 3.3 V device on a 3.3 V bus, so nothing is translated.
      connect L2.p2 -> J8.vdd
      connect J8.vss -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect J8.vdd -> C47.p1
      connect C47.p2 -> J8.vss as "GND"
      connect J8.vdd -> C48.p1
      connect C48.p2 -> J8.vss as "GND"
      connect J8.clk -> U5.io18 as "SPI_SCK" class "SPI_BUS"
      connect J8.cmd_mosi -> U5.io23 as "SPI_MOSI" class "SPI_BUS"
      connect J8.dat0_miso -> U5.io19 as "SPI_MISO" class "SPI_BUS"
      connect U5.io26 -> J8.dat3_cs as "SD_CS" class "SPI_BUS"

      // The SD specification wants the command and data lines pulled up so a
      // card that has not been selected does not float its outputs.
      connect J8.cmd_mosi -> R53.p1
      connect R53.p2 -> L2.p2
      connect J8.dat0_miso -> R54.p1
      connect R54.p2 -> L2.p2
      connect J8.dat3_cs -> R55.p1
      connect R55.p2 -> L2.p2

      // DAT1 and DAT2 are unused in one-bit mode and the socket's own
      // specification is that they may float; naming them keeps the pads
      // accounted for rather than silently absent.
      connect J8.dat1 -> R55.p2
      connect J8.dat2 -> R55.p2

      // The detect switch closes to ground when a card is seated, read on an
      // expander pin because the module has none to spare.
      connect U10.gpa6 -> J8.det_a as "SD_DETECT"
      connect J8.det_b -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect J8.shield -> U2.gnd as "GND"
    }

    group "INDUSTRIAL COMMS" color "#854d0e" {
      component U17: ic        "sn65hvd230_can"
      component C45: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF"
      component R48: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "10k"
      component R49: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "120"
      component J6: connector  "jst_ph_3pin" {
        placement_hint { region: right_edge edge: right priority: hard }
      }
      component U18: ic        "max3485_rs485"
      component C46: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF"
      component R50: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "120"
      component R51: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "560"
      component R52: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "560"
      component J7: connector  "jst_ph_3pin" {
        placement_hint { region: right_edge edge: right priority: hard }
      }

      // CAN, via the TWAI controller in the module. A 3.3 V transceiver, so
      // the logic side faces the module with nothing in between.
      connect L2.p2 -> U17.vcc
      connect U17.gnd -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U17.vcc -> C45.p1
      connect C45.p2 -> U17.gnd as "GND"
      connect U5.io32 -> U17.d as "CAN_TX"
      connect U17.r -> U5.sensor_vp as "CAN_RX"
      // Rs through 10k selects slope control rather than the fastest edge,
      // which is the right default for field wiring; grounded it would be
      // full speed and floating it would be standby.
      connect U17.rs -> R48.p1
      connect R48.p2 -> U17.gnd as "GND"
      connect U17.canh -> J6.p1 as "CANH" class "FIELDBUS"
      connect U17.canl -> J6.p2 as "CANL" class "FIELDBUS"
      connect J6.p3 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      // 120 ohm across the pair. This board is wired as a bus end; a node in
      // the middle of a segment must not fit R49, and the silkscreen says so.
      connect U17.canh -> R49.p1
      connect R49.p2 -> U17.canl

      // RS-485, half duplex on the second UART. RE is tied low so the
      // receiver is always on and the node hears its own transmission.
      connect L2.p2 -> U18.vcc
      connect U18.gnd -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U18.vcc -> C46.p1
      connect C46.p2 -> U18.gnd as "GND"
      connect U5.io33 -> U18.di as "RS485_TX"
      connect U18.ro -> U5.io35 as "RS485_RX"
      connect U18.re_n -> U18.gnd as "GND"
      // DE on IO2, which is a strapping pin that must be low or floating at
      // reset. Receive is the safe default and it is also what low means
      // here, so the two requirements are the same requirement.
      connect U5.io2 -> U18.de as "RS485_DE"
      connect U18.a -> J7.p1 as "RS485_A" class "FIELDBUS"
      connect U18.b -> J7.p2 as "RS485_B" class "FIELDBUS"
      connect J7.p3 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U18.a -> R50.p1
      connect R50.p2 -> U18.b
      // Fail-safe bias: without it an idle bus floats and the receiver
      // chatters. 560 ohm to each rail holds A above B by about 200 mV.
      connect U18.a -> R51.p1
      connect R51.p2 -> L2.p2
      connect U18.b -> R52.p1
      connect R52.p2 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
    }

    group "RTC AND SUPERVISION" color "#0f766e" {
      component U19: ic        "ds3231m_rtc"
      component C49: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF"
      component R56: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "10k"
      component BT1: battery   "coin_cell_cr2032" {
        placement_hint { region: bottom_edge edge: bottom priority: soft }
      }
      component U20: ic        "tps3839_supervisor"
      component C50: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF"

      // The M variant carries its own MEMS resonator, so there is no crystal
      // to place and no 32 kHz layout to get wrong.
      connect L2.p2 -> U19.vcc
      connect U19.gnd -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U19.gnd_2 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U19.gnd_3 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U19.gnd_4 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U19.gnd_5 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U19.gnd_6 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U19.gnd_7 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U19.gnd_8 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U19.gnd_9 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U19.vcc -> C49.p1
      connect C49.p2 -> U19.gnd as "GND"
      connect U5.io21_sda -> U19.sda as "I2C_SDA"
      connect U5.io22_scl -> U19.scl as "I2C_SCL"
      // The alarm output is open drain and needs a pull-up to be read.
      connect U10.gpa7 -> U19.int_sqw_n as "RTC_ALARM"
      connect U19.int_sqw_n -> R56.p1
      connect R56.p2 -> L2.p2

      // A CR2032 is not rechargeable, so it must never see a charging path.
      // It does not: VBAT is an input the part switches over to when VCC
      // falls below it, and there is nothing between the cell and that pin.
      connect BT1.plus -> U19.vbat
      connect BT1.minus -> U2.gnd as "GND"

      // The supervisor holds the module in reset until V3V3 is valid, and
      // through any brownout after. It watches the rail it runs on, so no
      // divider sets its threshold; the G33 option trips at 2.93 V falling.
      connect L2.p2 -> U20.vdd
      connect U20.gnd -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect U20.vdd -> C50.p1
      connect C50.p2 -> U20.gnd as "GND"
      connect U20.reset_n -> U5.en as "SYS_RESET"
    }

    group "DEBUG AND RECOVERY" color "#4c1d95" {
      component J9: connector "header_1x6" {
        placement_hint { region: top_edge edge: top priority: soft }
      }
      component LED5: led      "led_amber_0603"
      component R57: resistor  "r_generic_0603" value "1k"

      // The ESP32's JTAG port is fixed in silicon on IO12-15. Those four
      // pins carried the external flash on a dedicated HSPI bus until this
      // revision; the flash moved to the shared bus so that a debug port
      // could exist at all. There is no arrangement in which both have them.
      connect U5.io14 -> J9.p1 as "JTAG_TMS"
      connect U5.io13 -> J9.p2 as "JTAG_TCK"
      connect U5.io12 -> J9.p3 as "JTAG_TDI"
      connect U5.io15 -> J9.p4 as "JTAG_TDO"
      connect J9.p5 -> U2.gnd as "GND"
      connect L2.p2 -> J9.p6

      // IO12 is a strapping pin that selects the flash voltage at reset and
      // IO15 silences the boot log when held low, so a debugger must not
      // drive either during reset. The USB path stays the recovery route:
      // it needs no debugger and no jumper, which is what keeps a board with
      // bad firmware recoverable.

      // A comms-activity indicator on the last free expander pin.
      connect U10.gpb1 -> R57.p1
      connect R57.p2 -> LED5.anode
      connect LED5.cathode -> U2.gnd as "GND"
    }

    group "MECHANICAL" color "#525252" {
      component H1: mounting_hole "mounting_hole_m2_5"
      component H2: mounting_hole "mounting_hole_m2_5"
      component H3: mounting_hole "mounting_hole_m2_5"
      component H4: mounting_hole "mounting_hole_m2_5"
      component FID1: fiducial "fiducial_1mm"
      component FID2: fiducial "fiducial_1mm"
      component FID3: fiducial "fiducial_1mm"
    }

    // Copper must not sit under the module's antenna, which is at one end of
    // the package rather than at its centre.
    keepout antenna_clearance { radius 12mm }

    // Per pair, the intra-pair budget: 100BASE-TX tolerates a few hundred
    // picoseconds between halves, which is more FR4 than this board is wide,
    // so the binding constraint is the impedance and the coupling rather
    // than the skew. The budget is still declared, because an uncoupled pair
    // routed apart is the failure this catches.
    diff_pair ETH_TXP ETH_TXN {
      impedance 100ohm
      max_skew 0.3mm
      couple tight
    }

    diff_pair ETH_RXP ETH_RXN {
      impedance 100ohm
      max_skew 0.3mm
      couple tight
    }

    notes "Produced from a prompt via the Synth MCP server." {
      "A standalone computer, not a rail fixture: five rails from one 12 V"
      "input, an ESP32-WROOM-32 with all 39 pads modelled, its own USB-C"
      "programming path with auto-reset, QSPI flash, I2C EEPROM, 10/100"
      "Ethernet into a magjack, sixteen PWM channels, sixteen expander GPIO,"
      "a level-shifted 5 V header, and rail and die-temperature monitoring."
    }
}
